  { id:'opqb-t4-139', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'In **DCR**, the opening is made at:',
    options:['Superior meatus','Middle Meatus','Inferior meatus','Sphenopalatine fossa'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**(DCR)**: surgically connecting the lacrimal sac and the **middle meatus** of the nasal cavity with placement of either temporary or permanent tubes.\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Two different openings into the nose, and this topic asks about both.** The single commonest error on this question is to answer *inferior meatus* — which is the correct answer to `opqb-t4-146`, five questions later. They are not the same opening and they are not made by the same thing:\n\n| | Where it opens | Made by |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Nasolacrimal duct** (the natural passage) | **Inferior meatus**, under the inferior turbinate | Embryology |\n| **DCR stoma** (the surgical bypass) | **Middle meatus** | The surgeon |\n\nThe reason is anatomical, not arbitrary. **DCR bypasses the nasolacrimal duct altogether.** The whole point of the operation is that the duct is blocked, so the sac is opened directly into the nose through the bone of the lacrimal fossa. The sac lies in the lacrimal fossa on the **medial orbital wall**, and the piece of nasal cavity that sits immediately medial to it — behind the anterior lacrimal crest, at the level of the middle turbinate’s attachment — is the **middle meatus**. Cutting into the inferior meatus would mean drilling downwards along the very duct the operation exists to abandon.\n\n**The book prints both statements, on facing pages, and it prints them as a contradiction it never resolves.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.63: tears flow from the sac \"through the nasolacrimal duct into the nasal cavity, **behind the inferior turbinate**\"; p.66 again puts the NLD orifice \"under the inferior turbinate\". Then p.67 defines DCR as \"surgically connecting the lacrimal sad [sic] and the **middle meatus** of the nasal cavity with placement of either temporary or permanent tubes\" — which is, word for word, the sentence this bank has reprinted as its explanation box. So the bank is quoting the book directly, and the apparent conflict dissolves once you see that p.63 describes the **duct** and p.67 describes the **operation**.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What actually drains there |\n|---|---|\n| **Superior meatus** | The **posterior ethmoidal** air cells. Nothing lacrimal reaches it, and it is far too high and posterior to be reached from the lacrimal fossa. |\n| **Inferior meatus** | The **nasolacrimal duct** itself — the right answer to a different question (`opqb-t4-146`), and the trap here. |\n| **Sphenopalatine fossa** | Not a nasal meatus at all. It is the pterygopalatine fossa’s opening, transmitting the sphenopalatine vessels and nerves — a distractor that only sounds plausible because the word *sphenopalatine* belongs in the same region. |\n\n*(The meatal drainage assignments for the superior meatus and the sphenopalatine foramen are not taken from the course material — neither `L9) lacrimal system.` nor book ch.4 names any meatus except the inferior one, and \"middle meatus\" occurs in the whole ophthalmology cache only in the DCR sentence on book p.67. They are standard, uncontested nasal anatomy.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** DCR is the definitive operation for **nasolacrimal duct obstruction** and for **chronic dacryocystitis** — book p.68 gives it as the entire treatment of chronic dacryocystitis, and p.67 as the last rung of the congenital ladder. `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 29 lists only two forms, \"**External DCR**\" and \"**Endoscopic DCR**\", and describes neither. What must not be missed is when NOT to do it: see `opqb-t4-160`, where operating during acute dacryocystitis is the keyed contraindication.",
    objective:'DCR opens the lacrimal sac into the MIDDLE meatus; the natural nasolacrimal duct opens into the INFERIOR meatus — this bank asks both.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.21 (Q139), key p.24 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-140', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'**Schirmer’s test** is used for diagnosing:',
    options:['Dry eye','Infective keratitis','Watering eyes','Horner’s syndrome'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Schirmer’s test measures how much aqueous tear the lacrimal gland produces, so the disease it diagnoses is dry eye — specifically the aqueous-deficient type.** A strip of standardised filter paper is folded over the lower lid margin at the junction of its middle and lateral thirds and left for **five minutes**, and the length of paper wetted is read off in millimetres. Roughly **>10 mm** is normal, **5–10 mm** borderline and **<5 mm** clearly deficient. Performed **without** anaesthetic it measures basic plus reflex secretion (Schirmer I); **with** topical anaesthetic it approximates basic secretion alone.\n\n**⚠️ This is a gap in the course material and it is worth knowing precisely how big.** Searched case-insensitively across every cached ophthalmology deck and book chapter, the word *Schirmer* appears exactly **once as teaching**: `L15,16) Cornea` lists \"Adequate tear film (**Schirmer’s**, TBUT)\" as an item in the pre-refractive-surgery workup, and says nothing further. Everywhere else it appears only in a recorded absence — `L9) lacrimal system.` notes \"The **SCHIRMER TEST**. It appears on no slide, in no figure and in no caption\"; `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy…` records \"Schirmer = 0 hits\"; book ch.1 records \"**Schirmer — NOT PRINTED.** No tear-secretion test of any kind appears in this chapter\"; and book ch.4, the lacrimal chapter itself, lists it among terms \"verified absent\", noting that \"the chapter teaches **TBUT** as the dry-eye test and never mentions Schirmer\". *(The strip, the five minutes and the millimetre thresholds above are therefore **not taken from the course material** — they are the standard, uncontested description of the test. The bank asks about it anyway, which is why they are given.)*\n\n**What the material does teach for dry eye**, and what this question is really sitting beside: **tear film break-up time**. Book p.65 makes TBUT the sign — \"Decreased tear film breakup time (TBUT) normally **more than 10 seconds** (if less, it is an indication of an unstable tear film)\" — and this bank keys that same fact at `opqb-t4-162`. The two tests answer different questions, and `opqb-t4-149` is built entirely on the difference: **Schirmer measures quantity, TBUT measures stability.**\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What actually diagnoses it |\n|---|---|\n| **Infective keratitis** | Slit-lamp examination with **fluorescein** to size the epithelial defect, plus **corneal scraping** for Gram stain and culture. A Schirmer strip tells you nothing about an ulcer — although a dry eye is a **risk factor** for one, which is the loose association the distractor trades on. |\n| **Watering eyes** | The **drainage** tests, not a secretion test: the regurge test, the fluorescein dye disappearance test, probing and irrigation (book p.66), and Jones I and Jones II (`L9)` slides 18–19). This is the sharpest distractor, because a paradoxically **watering** eye is a classic presentation of dry eye — `L9)` slide 9 calls it \"Hypersecretion secondary to anterior segment disease such as dry eye (**‘paradoxical watering’**)\". But Schirmer does not diagnose the watering; it explains it. |\n| **Horner’s syndrome** | Pharmacological pupil testing — cocaine or apraclonidine to confirm, hydroxyamphetamine to localise. Horner’s is a **sympathetic** lesion and the lacrimal gland’s secretomotor supply is **parasympathetic**, so tear volume is essentially unaffected. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In a patient complaining of a gritty, burning, end-of-day eye, do both tests and read them together: a **low Schirmer** points to aqueous deficiency and its causes — Sjögren syndrome and the long non-Sjögren list in book p.64 — while a **normal Schirmer with a short TBUT** points to evaporative disease, which in practice means meibomian gland dysfunction, blepharitis or simply not blinking at a screen. The treatment ladder differs, and `opqb-t4-151` and `opqb-t4-157` test both ends of it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Schirmer’s test measures aqueous tear PRODUCTION and diagnoses dry eye; TBUT measures tear film STABILITY, and the drainage tests are what a watering eye needs.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.21 (Q140), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-141', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'3 months old infant with watering; lacrimal sac on pressing causes regurgitation of mucous material. What is the appropriate treatment?',
    options:['Dacryocystorhinostomy','Probing','Probing with syringing','Massage with antibiotics up to the age of 6 months'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Birth → 6 months’ treatment:** lacrimal sac massage **+Topical antibiotics if discharge is present**; if failed **probing**; if failed **repeat it once**; if failed **intubation of lacrimal passages**; if failed **DCR**.\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The whole question is the age.** A watering infant with mucoid material refluxing from the sac on pressure is **congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction** — the diagnosis is not in doubt and no option disputes it. What is being tested is where a **three-month-old** sits on the treatment ladder, and the answer is at the bottom rung: conservative massage, with topical antibiotic added only because there is discharge.\n\n**Why waiting is right, and it is a number worth memorising.** `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 21 explains that \"the lower end of the nasolacrimal duct, in the region of the **valve of Hasner**, is the last portion of the lacrimal drainage system to canalize\", that \"**epiphora affects at least 20% of neonates**\", and — the figure that decides management — that \"**spontaneous resolution occurs in approximately 85% within the first year**\". Book p.67 prints the same 85 % and attaches the ages: \"Digital massage of the lacrimal sac with topical antibiotics **before the age of one year** (85% resolve within the first year)\". Five in six of these babies get better with nothing but a finger, so an operation at three months would be an operation five-sixths of these children never needed.\n\n**The escalation ladder, which this bank asks four separate ways** (`opqb-t4-141`, `opqb-t4-143`, `opqb-t4-163`, `opqb-t4-164`):\n\n| Rung | Intervention | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Massage** of the lacrimal sac ± topical antibiotics if there is discharge | First, and for months |\n| 2 | **Probing** — a fine wire through the canaliculus to rupture the membrane at the valve of Hasner, then irrigation to confirm patency (`L9)` slide 22) | After the conservative period, **repeatable only once** (book p.67) |\n| 3 | **Silastic intubation** of the lacrimal passages, ± balloon dilatation | If the second probing fails |\n| 4 | **DCR** | If intubation fails |\n\nWhy massage works at all: `L9)` slide 22 says it \"has been suggested as a means of **rupturing a membranous obstruction by hydrostatic pressure**\" — the finger raises pressure inside the sac and blows out the imperforate membrane from above.\n\n**⚠️ A divergence, recorded and not corrected. The bank’s box sets the conservative window at SIX months; the book sets it at ONE YEAR.** The box heads its ladder \"Birth → 6 months’ treatment\" and option D says \"up to the age of **6 months**\", whereas book p.67 says massage \"**before the age of one year**\" and probing \"**after the age of one year**\", and `L9)` slide 21 gives resolution as 85 % \"within the **first year**\". `L9)` slide 22 states no age threshold at all — its own not-covered list records that \"the **AGE** at which probing should be performed for congenital NLDO… Slide 22 describes massage, probing and intubation **without giving any age threshold**\". The keyed answer is the bank’s and stands as printed; be aware that an examiner working from the book may say twelve months where this bank says six. Note also that the bank is internally consistent with itself: `opqb-t4-164` keys **probing** for a **14-month-old**, which satisfies both thresholds.\n\n**Why each of the other three is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not at three months |\n|---|---|\n| **Dacryocystorhinostomy** | The **last** rung, reached only after a failed intubation. It is a bone-opening operation on an infant with an 85 % chance of spontaneous cure. |\n| **Probing** | Correct, but **later** — see `opqb-t4-164`, where a 14-month-old with the identical picture is keyed to probing. Done at three months it treats a duct that would very probably have opened by itself. |\n| **Probing with syringing** | Same objection, plus one more: `L9)` slide 15 warns that \"lacrimal **irrigation** should be performed only after ascertaining punctal patency\" and that probing \"is **contraindicated in acute infection**\". Adding syringing does not make premature probing appropriate. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Mucoid or mucopurulent reflux on sac pressure in a baby is the expected finding in congenital NLDO, not a sign of failure — `L9)` slide 21 says plainly that \"gentle pressure over the lacrimal sac may cause **mucopurulent reflux**\" and that \"acute dacryocystitis is **very rare**\" in these infants. Teach the parent the massage, treat discharge topically, and review. The findings that would change the plan are a **bluish cystic swelling** at the medial canthus (congenital dacryocoele, `L9)` slide 23) or a genuinely **acute, tender, red** sac.",
    objective:'Congenital NLDO in a young infant is treated by lacrimal sac massage with topical antibiotics if there is discharge; 85 % resolve spontaneously, so probing, intubation and DCR all come later.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.21 (Q141), key p.24 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-142', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'The lacrimal **secretory** system consists of the following parts **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['The lacrimal gland.','The accessory lacrimal gland.','The goblet cells','The lacrimal sac'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The lacrimal sac is part of the EXCRETORY system, not the secretory one — it is a reservoir on the drainage route and it secretes nothing.** The division is the first thing book ch.4 teaches, on p.63:\n\n> **1. Secretory:** \"Composed of the **main lacrimal gland**, the **accessory lacrimal glands**, **conjunctival goblet cells** and the **Meibomian glands**.\"\n>\n> **2. Excretory:** \"Tears drain from the ocular surface through the upper and lower **lacrimal puncta** via the superior and inferior **canaliculi**… into the **lacrimal sac**. From the lacrimal sac, tears flow through the **nasolacrimal duct** into the nasal cavity, behind the inferior turbinate.\"\n\nBook ch.1 p.14 prints the same two-part scheme with the sac again on the excretory side, \"located in the lacrimal sac fossa in the anteroinferior nasal side of the orbit\".\n\n**Each secretory component makes one layer of the tear film** — which is why they are grouped together at all, and why `opqb-t4-159` and `opqb-t4-150` are the same anatomy asked from the other end:\n\n| Structure | Layer it makes |\n|---|---|\n| **Lacrimal gland** + **accessory glands** (Krause, Wolfring) | **Aqueous** — the bulk |\n| **Goblet cells** of the conjunctiva | **Mucin** — innermost |\n| **Meibomian glands** | **Lipid** — outermost |\n\n`L9) lacrimal system.` teaches this as two figure slides with no prose at all: slide 4, \"The Secretory Part / Aqueous part\", labels **Glands of Krause**, **Glands of Wolfring**, **Meibomian gland**, **Glands of Zeis**, **Glands of Moll** and **Glands of Manz** on a sagittal diagram; slide 5, \"The Secretory Part / Mucin and Lipid part\", shows the meibomian row through an everted lid and a map of goblet-cell density \"with eyelids everted\", denser **medially**.\n\n**Why the first three are all genuinely secretory:** the **lacrimal gland** is the main aqueous source, sitting in the lacrimal fossa in the upper temporal orbit (book ch.1 p.14); the **accessory lacrimal glands** are histologically identical, lie in the conjunctiva and fornices, and supply what is traditionally called basic secretion — book p.63 names them only as \"the accessory lacrimal glands in the conjunctiva\", and the eponyms **Krause** and **Wolfring** exist in this material only as labels on the figures of `L9)` slide 4 and `L1,2)` slide 53; the **goblet cells** secrete the mucin that, per book p.63, \"helps maintain the **adherence and stability** of the tear film on the ocular surface, especially the cornea\".\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The split is not bookkeeping — it sorts symptoms. A **secretory** failure gives a **dry, gritty, burning** eye. An **excretory** failure gives a **wet** one: epiphora. Both can water, which is the trap `opqb-t4-153` and `opqb-t4-149` are built on, but only the excretory side gives mucopurulent reflux on pressing the sac.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The secretory system is the lacrimal gland, accessory glands, goblet cells and meibomian glands; the puncta, canaliculi, sac and nasolacrimal duct are the excretory system.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.21 (Q142), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-143', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'The first line of treatment in a case of **congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction** during the 1st 4 months of life is:',
    options:['Massage and antibiotic eye drops','Probing and syringing.','Dacryocystectomy.','Dacryocystorhinostomy'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The same ladder as `opqb-t4-141`, asked at four months instead of three — and the answer is the same bottom rung: massage, with antibiotic drops for the discharge.** The full escalation table, the 85 % spontaneous-resolution figure, the mechanism by which massage works, and the recorded six-months-versus-one-year divergence between this bank and the book are all set out in `opqb-t4-141`; they are not repeated here.\n\n**What this printing adds is the word *first line*.** Book p.67 orders the rungs explicitly — (a) digital massage with topical antibiotics before the age of one year, (b) probing after the age of one year, (c) silastic intubation if a second probing fails, (d) DCR if intubation fails — and this question simply asks for (a).\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually is | Why not first line at four months |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Probing and syringing** | Rung 2 — a fine wire through the canaliculus to rupture the membrane at the valve of Hasner, then irrigation to confirm patency (`L9) lacrimal system.` slide 22) | Premature. The duct is still canalising and 85 % open by themselves within the first year (`L9)` slide 21) |\n| **Dacryocystectomy** | **Removal of the lacrimal sac** — not on the congenital ladder at all. It is a destructive operation reserved for a sac that cannot be salvaged or is the seat of a tumour | It abolishes drainage permanently in a child whose drainage is about to work |\n| **Dacryocystorhinostomy** | Rung 4, the definitive bypass into the middle meatus (`opqb-t4-139`) | Reached only after intubation has failed |\n\n*(Dacryocystectomy is **not taken from the course material** — the word appears in no cached ophthalmology deck and in no book chapter; `L9)` slide 29 lists only external and endoscopic DCR as operations for acquired obstruction. It is included here because the bank prints it as a distractor.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** \"Antibiotic\" in this answer is treating the **discharge**, not the obstruction — book p.67 pairs it with the massage precisely so, and `L9)` slide 21 lists \"superimposed **bacterial conjunctivitis**\" among the expected features. Drops will clear the discharge and it will come back, because the block that causes it is untreated. Say that to the parent at the first visit and the second visit is much easier.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'First-line treatment of congenital NLDO in the first months of life is lacrimal sac massage with antibiotic drops for any discharge — never probing, never surgery.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.21 (Q143), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-144', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'The most common causative organism in **chronic dacryocystitis** is',
    options:['Pneumococci.','Diphtheria bacilli.','Staphylococci.','None of the above'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The most common organisms of Dacrocystitis are staphylococci or streptococci.**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim, including its spelling of \"Dacrocystitis\". The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Both sources agree and both name the same pair.** Book p.67: \"The most common organisms are **staphylococci or streptococci**.\" `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 32: infection of the lacrimal sac \"is most commonly **staphylococcal or streptococcal**\", and — the sentence that explains why any organism gets a chance — \"infection of the lacrimal sac is usually **secondary to obstruction of the nasolacrimal duct**\". Stagnant tears in an obstructed sac are a culture medium; the organisms that grow are the ordinary skin and conjunctival flora.\n\n**⚠️ A defect in the option list, recorded and not corrected.** Both sources name **two** organisms, staphylococci *or* streptococci, and rank neither above the other; the bank offers **staphylococci** alone among the options and keys it. Since streptococci are not offered, C is the only defensible choice and the key is sound — but a candidate who has learned the pair correctly will hesitate over **\"None of the above\"**, which is the fourth option here. Answer C. Be ready for either organism to be the intended answer if an examiner offers both.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually causes in the eye |\n|---|---|\n| **Pneumococci** | *Streptococcus pneumoniae* is a genuine cause of bacterial **conjunctivitis** and of a **serpiginous corneal ulcer**, classically in an eye whose drainage is already blocked — so it is associated with dacryocystitis as a **consequence** rather than as its commonest cause. The strongest distractor. |\n| **Diphtheria bacilli** | *Corynebacterium diphtheriae* causes **membranous conjunctivitis**, historically important and now vanishingly rare. It is not a sac organism. |\n| **None of the above** | Only correct if staphylococci were wrong, and the bank’s own box names them first. |\n\n**Acute versus chronic — the distinction the next questions need.** `L9)` slide 32 and book pp.67–68 divide the disease sharply, and this bank tests both halves (`opqb-t4-154` acute, `opqb-t4-160` and `opqb-t4-148` chronic):\n\n| | Acute dacryocystitis | Chronic dacryocystitis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Onset | Subacute, over days | Long-standing |\n| Pain | **Very tender, tense**, red swelling; may be febrile | **Painless** |\n| Swelling | Progresses to **abscess**; preseptal cellulitis may follow | **Mucocele** at the inner canthus |\n| Sac pressure | \"Palpation is **painful and should be avoided**\" (`L9)` slide 12) | **Positive regurge test** — mucopurulent reflux |\n| Treatment | Warm compresses, systemic and topical antibiotics; DCR **after** it settles | **DCR** (book p.68) |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Culture is rarely what changes management here; **relieving the obstruction is**. Antibiotics will settle the episode and it will recur as long as tears stagnate in the sac — which is exactly why book p.68 gives DCR as the whole treatment of the chronic form, and why `opqb-t4-148` keys DCR rather than antibiotics for a woman with chronic tearing and mucopurulent reflux.",
    objective:'Dacryocystitis is most commonly staphylococcal or streptococcal, and it is secondary to nasolacrimal duct obstruction — so relieving the block, not the antibiotic, is the cure.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.21 (Q144), key p.24 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-145', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'**Nasolacrimal duct obstruction** commonly presents with:',
    options:['Dry eye','Blepharitis','Epiphora','None of the above'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**If tears cannot leave the eye, they spill over the lid margin — and that overflow is epiphora, the presenting symptom of every obstruction anywhere on the drainage route.** `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 9 defines it in one line: \"**Epiphora is the overflow of tears at the eyelid margin**\", and lists \"**obstruction at any point along the drainage system**\" as one of its two mechanisms. Book p.66 says the same with the mechanism folded in: epiphora is \"excessive watering of the eye with the overflow of tears at the lid margin **as a result of insufficient tear drainage**\".\n\n**The two roads to a watering eye**, which is the distinction the whole topic turns on:\n\n| Mechanism | What is wrong | Typical cause |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Hypersecretion** | Too many tears made, drainage normal | Ocular surface disease — dry eye (**paradoxical watering**), conjunctivitis, keratitis, corneal foreign body, entropion, trichiasis (book p.66) |\n| **Defective drainage** | Normal tears, nowhere to go | **Punctal** malposition or stenosis, **canalicular** obstruction, **NLD obstruction**, **lacrimal pump failure** |\n\n`L9)` slide 10 gives the three history questions that separate them at the bedside: ask about **ocular discomfort and redness** to exclude hypersecretion; drainage failure \"tends to be **exacerbated by a cold and windy environment** and to be least evident in a warm dry room\"; and \"a complaint of the tears **overflowing onto the cheek** is likely to indicate drainage failure rather than hypersecretion\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Dry eye** | The opposite complaint — a **secretory** failure. It is a real trap because a dry eye can water reflexly (`L9)` slide 9, \"paradoxical watering\"), but the eye in NLD obstruction has too **much** tear on the surface, not too little. |\n| **Blepharitis** | Lid margin inflammation. It is a cause of **evaporative** dry eye (book p.64) and of **primary punctal stenosis** (`L9)` slide 25 names \"chronic blepharitis\" as one of the two commonest causes), so it can lie **upstream** of an obstruction — but it is not how an obstructed duct presents. |\n| **None of the above** | Excluded the moment epiphora is on the list. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Epiphora is a symptom, not a diagnosis, and the work after hearing it is **localisation**: examine the punctum first — `L9)` slide 10 states that \"**punctal abnormality is the most common cause of lacrimal drainage failure**\" — then press the sac for reflux, then run the fluorescein dye disappearance test, then probe and irrigate. The bank tests that whole sequence at `opqb-t4-148`, `opqb-t4-152` and `opqb-t4-153`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Nasolacrimal duct obstruction presents with epiphora — overflow of tears at the lid margin — and the first task is to separate defective drainage from hypersecretion.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.21 (Q145), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-146', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'**Nasolacrimal duct** opens in:',
    options:['Upper nasal meatus','Middle nasal meatus','Inferior nasal meatus','Nasopharynx'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The nasolacrimal duct ends in the inferior meatus, under the inferior turbinate, at the valve of Hasner.** The material states it three separate times. Book p.63: tears \"flow through the nasolacrimal duct into the nasal cavity, **behind the inferior turbinate**\". Book p.66 again: obstruction anywhere \"from the puncta to the orifice of the nasolacrimal duct **under the inferior turbinate**\". Book ch.1 p.14: \"nasolacrimal duct (by which the tears are conveyed into the nasal cavity **under the inferior turbinate**)\". The words **inferior meatus** themselves appear as a figure label — on the ch.4 lacrimal-apparatus figure (\"**Inferior meatus of nose**\", alongside \"Lacrimal fold (valve of Hasner)\") and on `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy…` slides 53 and 54, where the drainage passage is drawn and every segment measured.\n\n**⚠️ Note where this is NOT taught.** `L9) lacrimal system.`, the lacrimal lecture itself, never names any meatus. Its slide 6 labels the **middle** and **inferior turbinates** and the **hiatus semilunaris** on the drainage diagram and leaves the duct’s opening unlabelled; its own not-covered list records that \"the **INFERIOR MEATUS** as the site where the nasolacrimal duct opens… the word ‘meatus’ appears on **no slide**\". The fact is in the book and in `L1,2)`, not in the lacrimal deck.\n\n**⚠️ Do not confuse this with `opqb-t4-139`.** Six questions earlier this same bank keyed the **middle** meatus — for the **DCR stoma**, which is a surgical opening made *because* the natural inferior-meatal route is blocked. The full comparison table is in `opqb-t4-139`. In one line: **duct → inferior meatus; DCR → middle meatus.**\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What is actually there |\n|---|---|\n| **Upper nasal meatus** | Drains the **posterior ethmoidal** cells. Far too high and posterior. |\n| **Middle nasal meatus** | Drains the **frontal sinus, anterior ethmoids and maxillary sinus** — and is where a DCR is opened, which is the trap. |\n| **Nasopharynx** | Behind and below the nasal cavity proper. Tears reach it eventually by running back along the floor of the nose, which is why patients taste bitter eye drops — but the duct does not open there. |\n\n*(The sinus drainage assignments for the superior and middle meatus are **not taken from the course material**; no cached ophthalmology source assigns any sinus to any meatus. They are standard nasal anatomy, given because the bank offers all three meatuses as options.)*\n\n**The dimensions of the passage**, for completeness, and note that the two sources disagree about how to divide it — recorded, not reconciled: `L9)` slide 6 gives **canaliculus 8–10 mm, lacrimal sac 12–15 mm, nasolacrimal duct 12–18 mm**, and calls these \"the only numerical anatomy in the deck\". `L1,2)` slide 53 subdivides instead — canaliculus **vertical 2 mm + horizontal 8 mm**, sac **fundus 3–5 mm + body 10 mm**, NLD **intraosseous 12.5 mm + medial 5.5 mm** (18 mm in total, which is the top of `L9)`’s range).\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The **valve of Hasner** at that inferior-meatal opening is the last part of the system to canalise, which is why an imperforate Hasner valve is the entire pathology of congenital NLD obstruction (`L9)` slide 21) and of congenital dacryocoele (slide 23), and why a probe passed from the punctum is aimed at exactly that membrane.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The nasolacrimal duct opens into the inferior meatus, under the inferior turbinate, at the valve of Hasner — the middle meatus is where a DCR is opened instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.21 (Q146), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-147', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'Defective lacrimal drainage can result in any of these conditions **EXCEPT**',
    options:['Chronic dacryocystitis','Stenosis of Lacrimal puncti','Trichiasis','Mucocele of lacrimal sac'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Trichiasis is misdirected eyelashes rubbing on the ocular surface. It is a lid disease, it lies entirely outside the drainage system, and nothing that happens to a blocked nasolacrimal duct produces it.** It belongs on the opposite side of the epiphora table: book p.66 lists \"ocular surface irritation (**entropion - trichiasis**)\" under **overproduction** of tears, i.e. trichiasis is a **cause** of a watering eye, by hypersecretion, not a consequence of failed drainage.\n\n**Why the other two named consequences really do follow from a blocked duct.** `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 32 supplies the chain in one sentence: \"Infection of the lacrimal sac is **usually secondary to obstruction of the nasolacrimal duct**.\" Tears stagnate behind the block, the sac becomes a reservoir, and it either becomes infected or simply distends:\n\n| Consequence | How the block produces it |\n|---|---|\n| **Chronic dacryocystitis** | Stagnant tears in a closed sac become infected — staphylococci or streptococci (`opqb-t4-144`). Presents with chronic epiphora, recurrent unilateral conjunctivitis and a **positive regurge test** (book p.68) |\n| **Mucocele of the lacrimal sac** | The sac fills with retained mucus and distends into a **painless swelling at the inner canthus** (book p.68; `L9)` slide 33 photographs it as \"Fig. 3.22 (A) Mucocoele\") |\n\n**⚠️ A defect in the question, recorded and not corrected: option B is also not a *result*.** \"Stenosis of lacrimal puncti\" is a **cause** of defective drainage, not a consequence of it — book p.66 lists punctal stenosis among the obstructions that produce epiphora, and `L9)` slide 10 goes further: \"**punctal abnormality is the most common cause of lacrimal drainage failure**.\" `L9)` slide 25 then gives punctal stenosis its own aetiology — \"the most common causes are **chronic blepharitis and idiopathic stenosis**\", with herpes simplex and zoster lid infection, radiotherapy, cicatrizing conjunctivitis, chronic topical glaucoma treatment and 5-fluorouracil — and none of those causes is *blocked drainage downstream*. On a strict reading, B and C are both correct answers to an EXCEPT stem. **The bank keys C and C is the better answer**, because a punctum is at least *part of* the lacrimal drainage apparatus whereas an eyelash is not, and because there is a real, if uncommon, route by which chronic mucopurulent discharge scars a punctum. Answer C.\n\n**Why not the fourth option:** *mucocele of lacrimal sac* is the textbook consequence of the block and is offered as the last option; it is not the exception.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Read \"epiphora\" as a question with two answers, and ask which way the arrow points. **Trichiasis, entropion, dry eye, conjunctivitis and keratitis point INTO the eye** — they irritate the surface and make more tears. **Punctal stenosis, canalicular obstruction, NLD obstruction and pump failure point OUT** — they stop tears leaving. Everting the lid and looking at the lashes takes five seconds and settles which list you are on.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Lid — trichiasis itself is taught as an eyelid disorder, but the question is testing the consequences of drainage failure.)*",
    objective:'Chronic dacryocystitis and a mucocele follow a blocked drainage system; trichiasis is a lid disorder that CAUSES watering by irritation and is never its result.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.21 (Q147), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-148', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'A 70-year-old woman complains of chronic tearing and discharge. **Irrigation of the lower canaliculus produces a mucopurulent regurgitation from the upper canaliculus** after distending the lacrimal sac. Which of the following statements is true?',
    options:['Jones one testing will reveal dye in the nose',
             'There is probably no common canalicular obstruction',
             'This condition is usually cured permanently by antibiotics',
             'DCR is the treatment of choice'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Reflux through the OTHER punctum, after the sac has filled, localises the block below the sac — this is nasolacrimal duct obstruction with chronic dacryocystitis, and the treatment is DCR.** Book p.66 prints the rule as a two-line table cell, and the whole vignette is written to satisfy it:\n\n| Irrigation result | Where the block is |\n|---|---|\n| Reflux of saline **through the same punctum** | **Canalicular** obstruction — the fluid never reached the sac |\n| Reflux **through the other punctum** | **Nasolacrimal** obstruction — the fluid crossed the common canaliculus, filled the sac, and had nowhere else to go |\n\nEvery word of the stem carries weight. *Irrigation of the lower canaliculus* — the fluid went in below. *After distending the lacrimal sac* — it got as far as the sac, so the canaliculi and the common canaliculus are patent. *Regurgitation from the upper canaliculus* — it came back out the only other way. *Mucopurulent* — the sac contains infected mucus, so this is not a bare obstruction but a **chronic dacryocystitis** on top of it. *Chronic tearing and discharge* in a 70-year-old completes it: book p.68 gives chronic dacryocystitis as \"chronic epiphora\" plus \"chronic or recurrent **unilateral** conjunctivitis\", and `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 29 names \"**idiopathic stenosis — by far the most common**\" cause of acquired NLD obstruction, which is the age-related process at work here.\n\n**Why DCR.** Book p.68 gives the treatment of chronic dacryocystitis in a single word: \"**Dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR)**\". `L9)` slide 29 gives the same for acquired NLD obstruction — \"**External DCR**\" and \"**Endoscopic DCR**\", with nothing else offered. The operation bypasses the blocked duct by opening the sac into the middle meatus (`opqb-t4-139`), which drains the reservoir and removes the reason the infection keeps coming back.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Jones one testing will reveal dye in the nose** | The opposite. `L9)` slide 18 gives Jones I verbatim: \"**Positive** recovery in the nose = patent lacrimal system\"; \"**Negative** recovery in the nose = lower duct obstruction or atony of the sac\". This duct is obstructed, so Jones I would be **negative** — no dye in the nose. |\n| **There is probably no common canalicular obstruction** | ⚠️ **In meaning this is TRUE, and the only thing that stops it being a second correct answer is a misprint.** The bank prints it as \"there is **no probably** a common canalicular obstruction\" — the words are transposed. Read as intended, the reflux through the *other* punctum proves the fluid crossed the common canaliculus, so a common canalicular block is indeed excluded, and `L9)` slide 19 confirms the pairing from the other side: on Jones II a **negative** result means \"**common canalicular obstruction**\" or \"canalicualr stenosis\" [sic]. Recorded here and not corrected; the key stays as printed at D, which is the option the question is really about. |\n| **This condition is usually cured permanently by antibiotics** | Antibiotics settle an episode; they do not open a duct. As long as tears stagnate the infection recurs, which is precisely why the book’s treatment of the chronic form is surgery and not a prescription. |\n\n**⚠️ Two things you must not do to this eye.** `L9)` slide 15: probing \"**is contraindicated in acute infection**\", and \"lacrimal **irrigation** should be performed only after ascertaining punctal patency\". `L9)` slide 12: \"in **acute** dacryocystitis, palpation is **painful and should be avoided**\". This patient is chronic and not acutely tender, which is why irrigation was legitimate here.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Irrigation is the test that **localises**, and it does so by which punctum the fluid comes back out of. Same punctum, blocked canaliculus, and DCR will not help — that eye needs intubation, trephination, or a Lester Jones tube (`L9)` slide 27). Other punctum, blocked duct, and DCR is the operation.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'On lacrimal irrigation, reflux through the OTHER punctum means nasolacrimal duct obstruction — and with chronic mucopurulent discharge that is chronic dacryocystitis, treated by DCR.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.21 (Q148), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-149', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'A 52-year-old woman complains of burning, gritty sensation, redness, and blurry vision toward the end of the day. Symptoms worsen with computer use. **TBUT = 5 seconds. Schirmer = 14 mm.** What is the MOST likely type of dry eye?',
    options:['Aqueous deficiency','Evaporative','Neurotrophic','Reflex tearing dysfunction'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Two numbers decide this question, and they point in opposite directions: the tear film is unstable but the tear volume is normal. That combination is evaporative dry eye.**\n\n| Number given | Normal | Reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| **TBUT = 5 s** | \"normally **more than 10 seconds**… if less, it is an indication of an **unstable tear film**\" (book p.65) | **Abnormal** — the film breaks up too fast |\n| **Schirmer = 14 mm** | roughly **>10 mm** in 5 minutes | **Normal** — the gland is producing plenty |\n\nEnough tear is being made, and it is not staying on the eye. The layer whose job is to stop it leaving is the **lipid** layer — book p.63: the outer oily layer is \"secreted by the **Meibomian glands**\" and \"its main function is to **prevent/decrease tear evaporation**\". That is why `opqb-t4-150` keys the lipid layer for this same patient.\n\n**The history says the same thing twice.** *Symptoms worsen with computer use* is almost a quotation of book p.64’s evaporative list: \"**Infrequent blinking, associated with staring at computer or video screens** (nowadays these are **the most frequent contributors to DED**)\" — with the tutorial-flagged names *computer vision syndrome* and *digital eye syndrome*. And *toward the end of the day* is the signature of an evaporative problem: the film is depleted by hours of poor blinking, so the worst hour is the last one, whereas an aqueous-deficient eye tends to be gritty from waking.\n\n**The two aetiological groups, from book p.64:**\n\n| | **1. Aqueous tear deficiency** | **2. Evaporative** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Problem | Not enough tear made | Enough made, lost too fast |\n| **Schirmer** | **Low** | **Normal** |\n| **TBUT** | May be low too | **Low** |\n| Causes named | **Sjögren syndrome** (KCS, autoimmune, primary or secondary to rheumatoid arthritis or SLE); sarcoid infiltration of the gland; allergies; ageing hormonal decline; pregnancy; thyroid; many drugs; chemical injury; eye surgery; **reduced corneal sensation** (contact lenses, trigeminal dysfunction, post-LASIK/PRK); diabetes; **vitamin A deficiency** | **Thyroid eye disease** with lid retraction, proptosis, infrequent blinking, lagophthalmos; **blepharitis**; **screen use**; environmental (dusty, windy, hot/dry); **neurologic** — stroke, **Bell’s palsy**, Parkinsonism; **exposure keratitis**; mask-associated dry eye (MADE); and, in its own tutorial box, **meibomian gland dysfunction / posterior blepharitis** |\n\nBook p.64 adds a third category — \"**3. Mixed aetiologies: common**\" — which is the honest answer in most real clinics, but the question asks for the **MOST likely** type and the two numbers separate them cleanly.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Aqueous deficiency** | Directly excluded by the **Schirmer of 14 mm**. This is the answer the question is built to catch, because \"dry eye in a 52-year-old woman\" reflexively suggests Sjögren — and Sjögren would give a **low** Schirmer, dry mouth and a rheumatological history, none of which is here. |\n| **Neurotrophic** | Not one of the book’s categories. It refers to an **anaesthetic** cornea from a trigeminal lesion — herpes simplex or zoster, diabetes, post-surgical. Book p.64 does list \"↓ corneal sensation\" but places it under **aqueous** deficiency, and the hallmark of a neurotrophic eye is that it does **not** hurt. This patient has burning and grittiness, so her corneal sensation is intact. |\n| **Reflex tearing dysfunction** | Not a category in either source. Reflex secretion is real — book p.63 divides tear secretion into \"**basic**: constant, to keep the ocular surface lubricated\" and \"**reflex**: associated with ocular irritation or emotion\" — but a failure of reflex tearing is not a recognised type of dry eye, and if it were it would show as a low Schirmer, since a Schirmer without anaesthetic measures basic **plus** reflex secretion. |\n\n*(The Schirmer threshold of about 10 mm is **not taken from the course material** — Schirmer’s test is named in the whole ophthalmology cache only once, in `L15,16) Cornea`’s pre-refractive-surgery workup, and is described nowhere. The 14 mm in this stem can only be read as \"normal\" against outside knowledge; see `opqb-t4-140`. The 10-second TBUT threshold, by contrast, is the book’s own, on p.65.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Do not treat \"dry eye\" — treat which kind. This patient needs the screen habits addressed, her lid margins examined for meibomian gland dysfunction, and lubricants; a Sjögren workup would find nothing. And note the diagnostic order the exam rewards: **TBUT abnormal + Schirmer normal = evaporative; both abnormal = aqueous-deficient or mixed.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A short TBUT with a normal Schirmer is evaporative dry eye — the lipid layer is failing; a low Schirmer would mean aqueous deficiency instead.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.22 (Q149), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-150', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'A 52-year-old woman has burning, grittiness and blurred vision worst at the end of the day, worsened by computer use, with **TBUT = 5 seconds and Schirmer = 14 mm**. Which tear film layer is most likely defective?',
    options:['Mucin layer','Aqueous layer','Lipid layer','All layers equally'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The lipid layer is the one that stops tears evaporating, so a tear film that breaks up in five seconds while the gland is producing normally is a lipid problem.** Book p.63 states the function in one line: the outer oily layer is \"secreted by the **Meibomian glands**\" and \"its main function is to **prevent/decrease tear evaporation**\". Take that layer away and a normal volume of tear evaporates off the surface before the next blink can spread it — which is exactly what a TBUT of 5 seconds against a normal of \"more than 10 seconds\" (book p.65) measures.\n\n*(This is the same patient as `opqb-t4-149`, where the reasoning from the two numbers to **evaporative** dry eye is set out in full, with the book’s aqueous-versus-evaporative aetiology table. The stem here has been restated from that question because the bank prints it as \"In the previous case…\" and the deck is shuffled.)*\n\n**The three layers, their sources and their jobs** — the table both this question and `opqb-t4-159` are drawn from:\n\n| Layer | Position | Secreted by | Function | Thickness (`L9)` slide 3) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Lipid** | Outermost, at the air interface | **Meibomian glands** | Prevents / decreases **evaporation** | **0.1 µm** |\n| **Aqueous** | Middle, the bulk | **Main lacrimal gland** + accessory glands | Lubrication, nutrition, the main volume | **8 µm** |\n| **Mucin** | Innermost, on the epithelium | **Conjunctival goblet cells** | Maintains **adherence and stability** of the film on the cornea | **0.2 µm** |\n\n⚠️ The thicknesses come from `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 3 and from `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy…` slide 56, which give **0.1 µm lipid, 8 µm aqueous** and differ on the mucin layer — `L9)` prints **0.2 µm**, `L1,2)` prints **0.02–0.05 µm**. Recorded, not reconciled. **Book ch.4 prints no thickness at all**; its own not-covered list records \"tear film layer **THICKNESSES** — ABSENT… no measurement of any kind\". Note also that the book’s prose numbers the layers oily-first while its figure draws them mucin-first outward from the cornea; the order on the eye is **mucin → aqueous → lipid**.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Mucin layer** | A mucin deficiency also shortens TBUT — the film cannot wet the epithelium and beads up — so this is the strongest distractor. But mucin loss means **goblet cell** loss, and the causes are cicatrising: **vitamin A deficiency**, Stevens–Johnson syndrome, ocular cicatricial pemphigoid, chemical burns, trachoma. None is in the history, and a mucin-deficient eye does not fluctuate with screen use and time of day. |\n| **Aqueous layer** | Directly excluded by the **Schirmer of 14 mm**, which is the number in the stem that measures exactly this layer. |\n| **All layers equally** | Nothing in the picture is global, and it would leave the two discordant numbers unexplained. The book does recognise \"**3. Mixed aetiologies: common**\" (p.64), but a question asking which layer is \"**most likely** defective\" is asking you to use the discriminating data, not to refuse to. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Lipid-layer disease is, in practice, **meibomian gland dysfunction** — book p.64 boxes it as \"+ Meibomian gland dysfunction (**Posterior blepharitis**)\". That is why the treatment of an evaporative dry eye starts at the lid margin: warm compresses, lid hygiene, treating blepharitis (book p.65 lists \"**Treatment of the cause: e.g., blepharitis**\" as its own third arm), and only then lubricants. Evert the lid and look at the meibomian orifices before writing for artificial tears.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The lipid layer, from the meibomian glands, prevents tear evaporation — so a short TBUT with a normal Schirmer localises the defect to it.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.22 (Q150), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-151', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'A 52-year-old woman has burning, grittiness, redness and blurred vision worst at the end of the day, worsened by computer use, with **TBUT = 5 seconds and Schirmer = 14 mm**. Best first-line management?',
    options:['Topical steroids','Artificial tears','Punctal occlusion','Oral doxycycline'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Artificial tears are the first thing on the book’s treatment list and the first thing this patient should be given.** Book p.65 orders the management of dry eye disease in three arms, and the very first item of the first arm is what this question wants:\n\n| Arm | Steps, in the book’s own order |\n|---|---|\n| **1. Medical** | (a) **Artificial tears** — with preservatives, or preservative-free in severe cases · (b) longer-acting **gel and ointment at bedtime** · (c) **Cyclosporine A 0.05%** drops, which increase tear production · (d) **topical steroids for short durations** to control the inflammatory component |\n| **2. Interventional** | (a) **Punctal plugs** to prevent tear drainage · (b) lid taping overnight or **tarsorrhaphy** in severe lagophthalmos |\n| **3. Treatment of the cause** | e.g. blepharitis |\n\nThe book prints one warning next to (a): \"**Long term use of artificial tears with preservatives should be avoided**\" — hence preservative-free drops once the patient is instilling them frequently.\n\n*(This is the same patient as `opqb-t4-149` and `opqb-t4-150`, restated because the bank prints \"In the previous case…\" and the deck is shuffled. ⚠️ Note that the bank’s \"previous case\" here means **Q149**, not the question immediately before it: `opqb-t4-150` is itself a back-reference to Q149 and asks about a tear film layer, not about a patient.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and all three are real treatments, further down the same list:**\n\n| Option | Where it actually belongs |\n|---|---|\n| **Topical steroids** | Book p.65 step 1(d), and only \"**for short durations**\". Steroids on an ocular surface carry cataract, raised intraocular pressure and infection risk; they are an adjunct for the inflammatory component of established disease, never the opening move in a mild case. |\n| **Punctal occlusion** | Book p.65 step 2(a) — an **interventional** measure that keeps what tear there is on the eye by blocking its exit. It is aimed at **aqueous deficiency**, and this patient’s Schirmer of 14 mm says her volume is normal; plugging her puncta treats the wrong half of the problem and risks turning a dry eye into a watering one. |\n| **Oral doxycycline** | A genuine and rational treatment for **meibomian gland dysfunction**, which is very likely what underlies this evaporative picture — but it is **not printed anywhere in book ch.4 or in `L9) lacrimal system.`**; the chapter’s only named drug strength is cyclosporine A 0.05 %. It is also a systemic antibiotic given for weeks to months, which is not a first line for a mild, screen-related complaint. *(The role of doxycycline in MGD is not taken from the course material.)* |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Start with lubricants and the cause — for this patient that means screen and blink advice and attention to the lid margins — and escalate only if she does not settle. The ladder in book p.65 is worth carrying whole, because examiners ask it at both ends: this question asks for the bottom rung, and `opqb-t4-157` asks which rung actually **increases tear production**.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'First-line treatment of dry eye is artificial tears; steroids, punctal plugs and systemic drugs are later rungs on the book’s ladder.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.22 (Q151), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-152', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'A 67-year-old man presents with chronic watering. **Fluorescein disappears normally in one eye but persists >5 min in the other.** What does this indicate?',
    options:['Normal drainage','Punctal stenosis','Nasolacrimal duct obstruction','Overproduction of tears'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**This is the fluorescein dye disappearance test, and dye still sitting in the conjunctival sac after five minutes means the tears are not draining.** `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 13 gives the test and its reading: the dye is instilled \"into both conjunctival sacs\", and \"**normally, little or no dye remains after 5–10 minutes. Prolonged retention is indicative of inadequate lacrimal drainage**\". Book p.66 prints the same rule in the only place it exists in that chapter — a red annotation baked into the photograph in the test table: \"After 5mins of dye instillation, the dye persists in the left conjunctival sac **indicating lacrimal drainage obstruction**\".\n\n**The design of the test is why the stem mentions both eyes.** Dye goes into both sacs and the two are compared, so the patient is his own control. One side clearing normally and the other holding dye is the finding; a bilaterally slow test in an elderly patient means much less.\n\n**⚠️ What the test does and does not tell you — and the bank has chosen the commonest site rather than the only possible one.** A delayed dye disappearance establishes that drainage is **inadequate**; it does **not** localise the block. Both sources say only \"inadequate lacrimal drainage\" / \"lacrimal drainage obstruction\", with no site attached. Of the options offered, **nasolacrimal duct obstruction** is the right answer because it is by far the commonest cause of acquired drainage failure in a 67-year-old — `L9)` slide 29 lists the causes of acquired NLD obstruction and heads them \"**Idiopathic stenosis – by far the most common**\" — but the localisation comes from the epidemiology, not from the test. Localising properly needs the next steps: press the sac (`opqb-t4-153`), then probe and irrigate (`opqb-t4-148`).\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Normal drainage** | Directly contradicted: normal is little or no dye left at 5–10 minutes. |\n| **Punctal stenosis** | A genuine possibility, and not a stupid one — `L9)` slide 10 says \"**punctal abnormality is the most common cause of lacrimal drainage failure**\", and `L9)` slide 25 gives primary punctal stenosis its own aetiology (chronic blepharitis and idiopathic stenosis commonest). But punctal stenosis is diagnosed by **looking at the punctum on the slit lamp**, which the stem does not report, and the bank keys the site further down. |\n| **Overproduction of tears** | The opposite mechanism. If tears were merely over-produced the drainage system would still clear the dye — dye retention is precisely how the test separates hypersecretion from drainage failure, which is its whole purpose. |\n\n**Where the test sits in the sequence.** `L9)` slides 10–15 give a fixed order and it is worth following: history (does it overflow onto the **cheek**? is it worse in **cold, windy** conditions?), then external examination of the punctum, then the **marginal tear strip** on the slit lamp \"**prior to any manipulation of the eyelids or instillation of topical medication**\" (normally **0.2–0.4 mm**), then the **regurge test** on the sac, then this dye disappearance test, then probing and irrigation — remembering that probing \"is **contraindicated in acute infection**\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Fluorescein persisting in the conjunctival sac beyond 5 minutes means inadequate lacrimal drainage; in an elderly patient the commonest site is the nasolacrimal duct.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.22 (Q152), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-153', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'A 67-year-old man with chronic watering has fluorescein that disappears normally in one eye but persists >5 min in the other. **Pressure over the sac produces no discharge, but watery reflux from the punctum.** What is the most likely diagnosis?',
    options:['Functional epiphora','Complete NLDO','Canalicular obstruction','Conjunctivitis causing reflex tearing'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**What the sac pressure adds is a negative: there is no mucopurulent content in the sac. The bank reads a patent but non-propelling system — a lacrimal pump failure, which is what \"functional epiphora\" means.**\n\n**Functional epiphora is in both sources, under that name and one other.** Book p.66 lists, among the causes of decreased tear drainage, \"**lacrimal pump failure (functional obstruction)** which may occur 2ry to: **lower lid laxity (aging process)** or **weakness of the orbicularis muscle** (e.g., facial palsy)\". `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 9 gives the identical entry: \"**Lacrimal pump failure**, which may occur secondarily to **lower lid laxity** or weakness of the orbicularis.\" A **67-year-old** is precisely the patient the book’s parenthesis \"(aging process)\" is describing — the lower lid loses tone, the punctum no longer sits in the tear lake, and the orbicularis no longer squeezes the sac effectively with each blink. The anatomy is open; the pump has stopped working. `L9)` slide 8 teaches that pump as three arrowed diagrams with no prose at all, and the deck’s own not-covered list records that \"the **LACRIMAL PUMP** mechanism explained in words\" is absent.\n\n**Why the negative regurge matters.** `L9)` slide 12 states the positive finding: \"**Mucopurulent discharge on compression** is indicative of a patent canalicular system, but with an obstruction distal to the lower end of the lacrimal sac.\" Book p.66 lists what a **+ve regurge** means — \"patent upper lacrimal system / NLD obstruction / **chronic dacryocystitis**\". No mucopurulent discharge means the sac is not acting as an infected reservoir, so chronic dacryocystitis is off the table, and the bank takes the merely watery return as the sac emptying rather than as a distended sac decompressing.\n\n**⚠️ A tension between two keys in this bank, recorded and not corrected.** The immediately preceding question, `opqb-t4-152`, keys the **same patient** as **nasolacrimal duct obstruction** on the strength of the delayed dye disappearance — and option B here, **\"Complete NLDO\"**, restates that key word for word. Read strictly, the two answers cannot both describe the same eye. The reconciliation the bank appears to intend is sequential: the dye test shows only that drainage is inadequate (`L9)` slide 13 says exactly that and localises nothing), and the sac pressure then **revises** the site upward, away from a duct block and towards a pump failure. Read the other way — that any reflux at all means fluid entered the sac and could not go on — option B is defensible, and a sac obstructed without infection would return clear watery fluid too. **The key is A and stays as printed; the discrepancy is recorded here, not disputed.** If an examiner presses, the discriminating test is irrigation, not palpation: see `opqb-t4-148`, where reflux through the *other* punchtum after the sac distends is what actually proves a duct block.\n\n**Why the other two are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Canalicular obstruction** | The block would be **above** the sac, so pressure on the sac could not produce reflux from the punctum at all — nothing would have got in. `L9)` slide 19 pairs this instead with a **negative Jones II**: \"common canalicular obstruction\" / \"canalicualr stenosis\" [sic]. Its causes, per `L9)` slide 27, are congenital, trauma, herpes simplex, drugs, irradiation and a membrane after chronic dacryocystitis. |\n| **Conjunctivitis causing reflex tearing** | A **hypersecretion** cause (book p.66, \"ocular surface inflammation (conjunctivitis - keratitis - corneal foreign body)\"), and it is excluded twice over: the eye is not described as red or uncomfortable — `L9)` slide 10 says enquiry \"about ocular discomfort and redness\" is what excludes hypersecretion — and reflex tearing does not delay dye disappearance, because the drainage route is open. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A watering elderly eye with a patent, quiet drainage system is a **lid** problem, and it is treated at the lid: correcting lower lid laxity or ectropion, not by opening the sac into the nose. Doing a DCR on a functional epiphora is the classic way to leave a patient still watering after an operation. `L9)` slide 26 gives the lid-side repertoire — retropunctal (Ziegler) cautery for pure punctal eversion, and medial conjunctivoplasty for medial ectropion.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Epiphora with a patent system and no mucopurulent reflux on sac pressure is functional epiphora — lacrimal pump failure from lower lid laxity or orbicularis weakness.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.22 (Q153), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-154', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'A 42-year-old woman presents with painful, red swelling **below the medial canthus**, very tender, with fever. Best immediate management?',
    options:['Probing','DCR','Systemic antibiotics + warm compress','Topical lubricants'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A tender, tense, red swelling below the medial canthus with fever is acute dacryocystitis, and the immediate treatment is medical: warm compresses and antibiotics. Surgery comes later, and instrumentation now is contraindicated.**\n\n**The site is the diagnosis.** Book p.67 gives acute dacryocystitis as \"pain, redness and swelling over the lacrimal sac **below the medial canthal area**\", with epiphora, possible fever, and signs of a \"**very tender and tense swelling with overlying skin erythema**\" that \"can progress to **abscess** formation\", with preseptal cellulitis. `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 32 says the same: \"a very tender, tense red swelling develops at the medial canthus, commonly progressing to abscess formation. There may be associated preseptal cellulitis.\" **Below** the medial canthal tendon is where a lacrimal sac swelling sits, and it is the single most useful discriminator from a lesion above the tendon.\n\n**The treatment, in the sources’ own words:**\n\n| Source | What it prescribes |\n|---|---|\n| Book p.67 | \"**Warm compresses and nasal decongestants** · **Systemic and topical antibiotics** · Once the infection resolves, **consider dacryocystorhinostomy DCR** · For abscess: **incision & drainage** (Tutorial)\" |\n| `L9)` slide 32 | \"Initial treatment involves the application of **warm compresses and oral antibiotics** · **Irrigation and probing should NOT be performed** · **Incision and drainage** may be considered if pus points and an abscess is about to drain spontaneously · **Dacryocystorhinostomy is commonly required AFTER the acute infection has been controlled**\" |\n\nNeither source names a drug or a dose; book ch.4’s only printed strength anywhere is cyclosporine A 0.05 %.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Probing** | **Explicitly contraindicated.** `L9)` slide 32: \"irrigation and probing should not be performed\"; slide 15: probing \"is **contraindicated in acute infection**\"; slide 12: \"in acute dacryocystitis, **palpation is painful and should be avoided**\". Instrumenting an infected sac risks spreading infection into the orbit. |\n| **DCR** | The right operation, at the wrong moment. `L9)` slide 32 places it \"**after** the acute infection has been controlled\", and this bank keys that timing explicitly at `opqb-t4-160`, whose printed box says surgery in the acute phase is contraindicated because of \"increased risk of intraoperative bleeding + infection can spread to orbit or face + poor healing\". |\n| **Topical lubricants** | Treatment for a **dry** eye, not an infected sac. It does nothing for either the infection or the obstruction underneath it. |\n\n**Do not miss the underlying block.** `L9)` slide 32 opens with the sentence that governs the whole condition: \"infection of the lacrimal sac is **usually secondary to obstruction of the nasolacrimal duct**\". Antibiotics settle this episode; the duct is still shut, and it will happen again. That is why both sources bring the patient back for DCR once the eye is quiet.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Look for the two things that change the plan today: **pointing pus**, which brings forward incision and drainage, and **spread past the septum** — lid oedema, chemosis, proptosis, restricted movement or reduced vision — which turns preseptal cellulitis into orbital cellulitis and means admission and intravenous antibiotics.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Acute dacryocystitis is treated with warm compresses and systemic antibiotics; probing and irrigation are contraindicated and DCR is deferred until the infection has settled.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.22 (Q154), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-155', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'A 19-year-old male presents with a 2-day history of severe pain in the **superolateral orbit**, **S-shaped ptosis**, redness, and fever. What is the most common infectious cause?',
    options:['Staphylococcus aureus','EBV','Herpes zoster','CMV'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Every element of this stem localises to the lacrimal gland, and the commonest infectious cause of acute dacryoadenitis is viral — Epstein–Barr virus.** Book p.68 states the ranking directly: under Aetiology, \"**Infections** — **Most commonly viral Epstein-Barr virus.** Less common viral causes include adenovirus, varicella zoster, herpes simplex, rhinovirus, cytomegalovirus, or mumps. **Less commonly bacterial** and rarely fungal or parasitic.\"\n\n**Why the picture is a lacrimal gland and nothing else.** The gland sits in the lacrimal fossa in the **upper temporal** orbit (book ch.1 p.14) and book p.68 places dacryoadenitis in \"the **outer one-third of the upper lid**\". Book p.69 lists the acute features and the stem repeats them almost item for item — \"pain in the **superolateral** part of the orbit aggravated with eye movements\", \"drooping of the upper lid\", \"**red eye**\", \"**fever and malaise** may be present\", and the sign that gives the game away: \"**Ptosis with S-shaped deformity of the upper lid**\". `L10) orbit` slide 31 explains the mechanics of that S: \"swelling of the **lateral aspect of the eyelid** overlying the **palpebral lobe** leads to a characteristic **S-SHAPED PTOSIS**\", with enlargement of the orbital lobe giving \"a slight **downward and inward dystopia**\". The lid droops more laterally than medially, so its margin traces an S rather than a smooth curve.\n\n**⚠️ The two sources rank the organisms differently — recorded, not reconciled.** Book p.68 says \"most commonly viral **Epstein-Barr virus**\" and this bank keys that. `L10) orbit` slide 31, the lecture that also teaches dacryoadenitis, lists it without ranking: \"**Idiopathic or due to viral (e.g. mumps, Epstein–Barr, cytomegalovirus**\" — mumps first, and idiopathic ahead of all of them. The book is the module’s main reference and the bank follows it; if an examiner working only from `L10)` asks the same question, mumps or \"idiopathic\" would not be a wrong thing to have in mind.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Where it really belongs |\n|---|---|\n| **Staphylococcus aureus** | The commonest **bacterial** cause — but book p.68 puts bacteria below viruses (\"less commonly bacterial\"). This is the intended trap: the fever and the two-day onset feel bacterial. Bacterial dacryoadenitis does exist and it is the one that \"will require **systemic broad-spectrum antibiotics**\" (book p.69). |\n| **Herpes zoster** | Book p.68 names **varicella zoster** among the \"less common viral causes\". Zoster ophthalmicus has its own unmistakable picture — a dermatomal V1 rash and, in a 19-year-old, would raise immunosuppression. |\n| **CMV** | Also in the book’s \"less common\" list, and in `L10)` slide 31’s trio. CMV disease in an immunocompetent 19-year-old is uncommon. |\n\n**How this is investigated and treated**, from book p.69: **CT or MRI of the orbits with contrast** (this bank keys that at `opqb-t4-156`), **chest X-ray if sarcoidosis is suspected**, no specific laboratory tests except to find an underlying cause, and biopsy for atypical or refractory disease. Treatment is **symptomatic for viral** disease, **systemic broad-spectrum antibiotics for bacterial**, **oral corticosteroids** especially in idiopathic disease, and treating the underlying disorder if chronic — with hot fomentation and analgesics/antipyretics in its tutorial box.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Acute dacryoadenitis in a young person is usually viral and usually self-limiting, but book p.68 attaches a warning to the diagnosis itself: \"**it may be a sign of a more serious pathology underlying the inflammation**\". A **chronic**, painless lacrimal gland enlargement is a different animal — book p.69 notes chronic disease is \"more common\" — and demands imaging and consideration of sarcoid, thyroid eye disease, IgG4 disease, lymphoma and lacrimal gland tumours, which `L10) orbit` teaches separately.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here. *(Secondary chapter: Orbit — `L10) orbit` teaches dacryoadenitis among the orbital infections, but the book files it in the lacrimal chapter and so does this bank.)*",
    objective:'Superolateral orbital pain with S-shaped ptosis is acute dacryoadenitis; the book’s commonest infectious cause is viral — Epstein–Barr virus — with bacteria less common.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.22 (Q155), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-156', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'A 19-year-old male has a 2-day history of severe pain in the superolateral orbit, S-shaped ptosis, redness and fever. What is the best initial imaging?',
    options:['Orbital X-ray','CT orbit','Sinus ultrasound','Skull X-ray'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Investigations of dacroadenitis:**\n\n**1. (CT) scan or (MR!) of the orbits with contrast.**\n\n**Evaluates** lacrimal gland enlargement · **Rules out** orbital cellulitis, **abscess, or sinus involvement** +anatomical details **for potential surgical planning**\n\n**2. X-ray Chest** if sarcoidosis is suspected.\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim, including its \"dacroadenitis\" and its \"(MR!)\" for MRI. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The box is the book’s own list.** Book p.69, Investigations: \"**Computed tomography (CT) scan or magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) of the orbits with contrast** · **X-ray Chest** if sarcoidosis is suspected · There are no specific laboratory tests (only done to diagnose an underlying cause e.g. autoimmune diseases) · **Biopsy** for tissue diagnosis for atypical infections or refractory disease.\" Of the four options offered, only CT is on that list at all.\n\n*(This is the same patient as `opqb-t4-155`, restated because the bank prints \"In the previous case…\" and the deck is shuffled. The reasoning from superolateral pain and S-shaped ptosis to acute dacryoadenitis is set out there.)*\n\n**Why CT rather than the alternatives, and what it is actually being asked to exclude.** The box names the reason: not to confirm a swollen lacrimal gland — which the examining finger has already found — but to **rule out orbital cellulitis, abscess or sinus involvement**. Those are the things that turn a self-limiting viral gland inflammation into an admission. CT shows bone, sinuses, orbital soft tissue and a collection all in one acquisition, is fast, and is available out of hours, which is why it is the **initial** study even though the book offers MRI as an equal alternative for the gland itself.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Orbital X-ray** | Plain film shows bone and almost nothing else. It cannot demonstrate a swollen lacrimal gland, an abscess or orbital soft tissue, and it is not in the book’s list. Historically used for radio-opaque intraorbital foreign bodies, and largely superseded even there. |\n| **Sinus ultrasound** | Not an investigation this material recognises anywhere. Ultrasound has real ocular uses — B-scan for a vitreous haemorrhage or a detached retina behind opaque media — but bone-enclosed sinuses are not visible to it. |\n| **Skull X-ray** | Even less targeted than an orbital film, and absent from every list in both sources. |\n\n*(The comparative reasoning above — CT’s speed and availability, ultrasound’s inability to see through bone, the residual role of plain films — is **not taken from the course material**; book p.69 states which studies to order and gives no rationale, and `L9) lacrimal system.` records that \"**CT or MRI of the lacrimal system**. Neither is mentioned anywhere\" in its 33 slides.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Imaging in an acutely inflamed orbit answers one question — **is there something that needs draining or admitting?** `L10) orbit` slide 48 prints the whole differential of an acutely inflamed orbit that a scan has to arbitrate between: bacterial orbital cellulitis, fungal orbital infection, dacryocystitis and infective dacryoadenitis; acute orbital haemorrhage, cavernous sinus thrombosis, carotid–cavernous fistula; rapidly progressive retinoblastoma, lacrimal gland tumour, rhabdomyosarcoma and leukaemia in children; rapid-onset thyroid eye disease; and idiopathic orbital inflammatory disease, Tolosa–Hunt syndrome and orbital myositis. That list is the reason nobody treats an inflamed orbit on inspection alone.",
    objective:'Acute dacryoadenitis is imaged with contrast CT (or MRI) of the orbits — chiefly to exclude orbital cellulitis, abscess or sinus involvement — with a chest X-ray if sarcoidosis is suspected.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.22 (Q156), key p.24 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-157', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'A 60-year-old man with long-standing **rheumatoid arthritis** complains of severe dryness, mucous strands, and punctate epithelial erosions on staining. Which medication used here **increases tear production**?',
    options:['Cyclosporine A 0.05%','Artificial tears','Steroid drops','Punctal plug'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Only one item on the book’s dry-eye list is described as increasing tear production, and the book says so in a parenthesis: \"Cyclosporine A 0.05% eye drops (increase tear production)\" — book p.65, treatment step 1(c).** It is also the only drug strength printed anywhere in book ch.4.\n\n**The vignette is textbook secondary Sjögren syndrome.** Book p.64 puts Sjögren syndrome (keratoconjunctivitis sicca) at the head of **aqueous tear deficiency**, notes it \"has an autoimmune aetiology\", is \"characterised by dry mucous membranes throughout the body\", and \"can be either **primary or secondary to a variety of rheumatological conditions (e.g., Rheumatoid arthritis, systemic lupus erythematosus)**\". The findings match the book’s own signs list on p.65: \"ocular surface staining with fluorescein sodium dye will show **punctate epithelial erosions (superficial punctate keratopathy [SPK])** in severe cases\", with \"mucous discharge\" among the symptoms. This is aqueous deficiency, not evaporative disease — the opposite pole from `opqb-t4-149`.\n\n**How cyclosporine differs from everything else offered.** It is a topical immunomodulator; it suppresses the T-cell-mediated inflammation of the lacrimal gland and conjunctiva that is causing the deficiency, and so it treats the **cause of the low output**. That is why the book’s parenthesis singles it out. Everything else on the list manages the consequences. *(The immunological mechanism is **not taken from the course material** — book p.65 states the effect and gives no mechanism.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — all three are real treatments, and none increases production:**\n\n| Option | What it does | Book p.65 |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Artificial tears** | **Replaces** tear from a bottle. Supplements volume; the gland makes no more than before | Step 1(a) — first line (`opqb-t4-151`), with \"long term use… with preservatives should be avoided\" |\n| **Steroid drops** | **Suppresses inflammation** for symptom control | Step 1(d), and explicitly \"**for short durations**\" only — cataract, raised IOP and infection risk on long use |\n| **Punctal plug** | **Retains** the tear already made by blocking its exit | Step 2(a), \"punctal plugs insertion (**to prevent tear drainage**)\" — an interventional measure, and note it is *conservation*, the exact opposite of *production* |\n\nThe punctal plug is the sharpest distractor because it genuinely raises the amount of tear **on the eye** — but the question asks what increases tear **production**, and the book’s own wording separates the two.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In aqueous deficiency the order runs: lubricate freely (preservative-free when frequent), add a gel or ointment at bedtime, treat the inflammation with cyclosporine and a short steroid course, and occlude the puncta when the volume is still not enough — plus treat the systemic disease. `L9) lacrimal system.` contributes nothing here; dry eye appears in that deck only as two passing phrases (slide 9’s \"dry eye ('paradoxical watering')\" and slide 24’s \"symptoms of dry eye\"), and Sjögren syndrome is recorded in its not-covered list as absent. **The book is the source for this whole question.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Cyclosporine A 0.05% drops are the treatment that increases tear production; artificial tears replace, steroids suppress inflammation, and punctal plugs merely retain.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.23 (Q157), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-158', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'Which of the following causes **evaporative** dry eye?',
    options:['Vitamin A deficiency','Facial nerve palsy','Goblet cell destruction','Low Schirmer test'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A facial nerve palsy paralyses the orbicularis oculi, the lids no longer close, and the exposed ocular surface dries by evaporation.** Book p.64 puts it on the evaporative list twice over — \"**Neurologic conditions e.g., stroke, Bell’s palsy, Parkinsonism**\" and \"**Exposure keratitis (eg., lagophthalmos)**\" — and it heads the same list with \"Thyroid eye disease with lid retraction, proptosis, **infrequent blinking and/or lagophthalmos**\". Every entry there works by the same mechanism: the tear film is either exposed for too long or not re-spread often enough.\n\n**The blink is what makes the tear film work**, and the seventh nerve is what makes the blink. Lose it and three things fail at once: the lids do not meet, so a strip of cornea — classically the **lower third** — stays uncovered; the film is not re-spread, so the lipid layer is never renewed across the surface; and the lacrimal pump stops, which is why book p.66 also lists \"weakness of the orbicularis muscle (**e.g., facial palsy**)\" as a cause of **functional epiphora**. A seventh nerve palsy can therefore give a patient a dry eye and a watering eye at the same time.\n\n**The two aetiological columns, from book p.64** — the discrimination the whole question rests on:\n\n| **Aqueous deficient** (not enough made) | **Evaporative** (lost too fast) |\n|---|---|\n| Sjögren syndrome (primary, or secondary to rheumatoid arthritis, SLE); sarcoid gland infiltration; allergies; ageing hormonal decline; pregnancy; thyroid; drugs (psychiatric, OTC cold remedies, antihistamines, beta-blockers, analgesics, hypnotics, diuretics, HRT, oral contraceptives); chemical injury; eye surgery; **reduced corneal sensation** (contact lenses, trigeminal dysfunction, post-LASIK/PRK); diabetes; **vitamin A deficiency** | Thyroid eye disease with lid retraction, proptosis, lagophthalmos; **blepharitis**; **infrequent blinking at screens** — \"the most frequent contributors\"; environmental (dusty, windy, hot/dry); **neurologic — stroke, Bell’s palsy, Parkinsonism**; **exposure keratitis**; mask-associated dry eye; and, boxed separately, **meibomian gland dysfunction / posterior blepharitis** |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Vitamin A deficiency** | Book p.64 lists it in the **aqueous deficiency** column, last item. (Classically it also destroys goblet cells and so causes **mucin** deficiency, xerophthalmia and Bitot’s spots — either way, not evaporative.) |\n| **Goblet cell destruction** | A **mucin** deficiency. The film loses its ability to wet and adhere to the epithelium — book p.63 gives the mucous layer’s job as maintaining \"the **adherence and stability** of the tear film on the ocular surface\". It shortens TBUT, which is why it feels like the evaporative answer, but the layer that fails is the innermost, not the outermost. |\n| **Low Schirmer test** | Not a cause of anything — it is a **result**, and a result that points the other way. A low Schirmer defines **aqueous deficiency** (`opqb-t4-140`, `opqb-t4-149`), and evaporative disease characteristically has a **normal** Schirmer. |\n\n*(The goblet-cell and xerophthalmia detail attached to vitamin A is **not taken from the course material** — book p.64 lists vitamin A deficiency without a mechanism and `L9) lacrimal system.` records dry eye as a topic absent from the deck.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A facial palsy is not just a dry eye — it is a cornea at risk, because the exposed strip goes on to break down. The measures are mechanical and they matter: lubricants by day, **ointment and lid taping** overnight, and **tarsorrhaphy** if closure cannot be achieved. Book p.65 lists \"lid tapping overnight or **tarsorrhaphy**… in severe cases of lagophthalmos\" among the interventional treatments.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Facial nerve palsy causes evaporative dry eye through lagophthalmos and lost blinking; vitamin A deficiency and goblet cell loss are aqueous and mucin problems, and a low Schirmer is a result, not a cause.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.23 (Q158), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-159', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'Which gland produces the tear film **oily layer**?',
    options:['Lacrimal gland','Goblet cells','Meibomian glands','Glands of Krause'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The meibomian glands make the lipid layer.** Book p.63 opens its account of the tear film with it: \"**1. Outer oily layer:** secreted by the **Meibomian glands**. Its main function is to **prevent/decrease tear evaporation**.\" `L9) lacrimal system.` teaches the same on slide 5, \"The Secretory Part / Mucin and Lipid part\", as a photograph of an everted lid margin showing the row of meibomian glands through the tarsal conjunctiva — the slide carries no prose at all, only the heading and the pictures.\n\n**Where the glands are:** modified sebaceous glands embedded vertically in the **tarsal plate**, about 30–40 in the upper lid and 20–30 in the lower, opening as a row of orifices along the lid margin behind the lashes. `L9)` slide 4 and `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy…` slide 53 label them on the sagittal lid diagram alongside the glands of Zeis and Moll. *(The gland counts and the tarsal position are **not taken from the course material**; both sources label the gland on a figure and do not describe it.)*\n\n**The three layers and their sources** — the same table as `opqb-t4-150`, which carries the thicknesses and the discrepancy between the two decks:\n\n| Layer | Made by | Function |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Lipid** (outer) | **Meibomian glands** | Prevents evaporation |\n| **Aqueous** (middle, the bulk) | **Main lacrimal gland** + accessory glands | Volume, lubrication |\n| **Mucin** (inner) | **Conjunctival goblet cells** | Adherence and stability on the cornea |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — each makes a different layer, which is the point of the option list:**\n\n| Option | What it actually secretes |\n|---|---|\n| **Lacrimal gland** | The **aqueous** layer — book p.63, \"secreted from the main lacrimal gland as well as the accessory lacrimal glands in the conjunctiva\", and it \"forms the main bulk of the tear film\" |\n| **Goblet cells** | The **mucin** layer — book p.63, \"secreted by the conjunctival goblet cells\". `L9)` slide 5 maps their distribution across the everted conjunctiva and shows them denser **medially** |\n| **Glands of Krause** | **Accessory lacrimal** glands, so also **aqueous**. They exist in this material only as figure labels — `L9)` slide 4 (circled in red by the lecturer) and `L1,2)` slide 53, alongside the glands of **Wolfring**, **Zeis**, **Moll** and **Manz**. ⚠️ Neither eponym is printed anywhere in book ch.4 or ch.1, which name only \"the accessory lacrimal glands in the conjunctiva\" |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Meibomian gland disease is the commonest cause of evaporative dry eye — book p.64 boxes \"**Meibomian gland dysfunction (Posterior blepharitis)**\" beside its evaporative list — and it is diagnosed by looking at the **lid margin**, not the cornea: capped or plugged orifices, thickened turbid secretion, telangiectasia along the margin. The treatment is the lid margin too: warm compresses, expression, hygiene. Note also which gland this is **not**: a chronic granulomatous inflammation of a blocked meibomian gland is a **chalazion**, while an infection of a lash follicle with its gland of Zeis or Moll is an external hordeolum.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The meibomian glands make the outer oily layer that prevents evaporation; the lacrimal and accessory glands make the aqueous layer and goblet cells make the mucin layer.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.23 (Q159), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-160', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'One of the following is **not** an indication for **dacryo-cysto-rhinostomy**:',
    options:['Lacrimal fistula.','Acute dacryocystitis.','Chronic dacryocystitis.','Mucocele of the lacrimal sac.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"In **Acute dacryocystitis**, **Surgery** during the acute phase is **contraindicated** because: increased risk of intraoperative bleeding **+Infection can spread to orbit or face +poor healing**.\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Acute dacryocystitis is not a contraindication to DCR forever — it is a contraindication to DCR *now*.** Both sources are explicit that the operation follows the infection rather than replacing it. `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 32: \"**Dacryocystorhinostomy is commonly required AFTER the acute infection has been controlled**\", with initial treatment being warm compresses and oral antibiotics, and \"irrigation and probing should **not** be performed\". Book p.67 orders the same: warm compresses and nasal decongestants, systemic and topical antibiotics, and \"**once the infection resolves, consider dacryocystorhinostomy DCR**\". The bank keys the acute phase and prints its three reasons — bleeding, spread, poor healing — which are the general surgical objections to operating through infected, hyperaemic, oedematous tissue.\n\n**Why the other three genuinely are indications:**\n\n| Option | Why DCR is indicated |\n|---|---|\n| **Chronic dacryocystitis** | The clearest of the four. Book p.68 gives the whole treatment of chronic dacryocystitis as one word: \"**Dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR)**\". Antibiotics cannot cure it because the obstruction that causes the stasis is still there (`opqb-t4-148`) |\n| **Mucocele of the lacrimal sac** | A mucocele *is* the chronic form — book p.68 lists \"painless lacrimal sac swelling (**mucocele**) at the inner canthus\" among its symptoms, and `L9)` slide 32 says the mucocele \"is usually evident as a painless swelling at the inner canthus\". Same disease, same operation |\n| **Lacrimal fistula** | A fistula is a track from a chronically obstructed, distended sac out through the skin. It cannot close while the sac has no other exit, so the treatment is to give the sac a route into the nose. `L9)` slide 33 photographs both stages — \"Fig. 3.21 (C) lacrimal **fistula** with fluorescein; (D) **healed** lacrimal fistula\" — without discussing them, and the deck’s own not-covered list records that congenital lacrimal fistula \"is photographed… and **never discussed**\". *(The management of a lacrimal fistula by DCR is therefore **not taken from the course material**; no cached ophthalmology source states an indication for it. It is standard oculoplastic practice.)* |\n\n**⚠️ Note what \"not now\" does not mean.** An acutely infected sac that has formed an **abscess** still gets surgery — but the right operation is **incision and drainage**, not DCR. `L9)` slide 32: \"incision and drainage may be considered **if pus points and an abscess is about to drain spontaneously**\"; book p.67 lists \"for abscess: incision & drainage\" (tutorial-flagged). Draining an abscess is not opening a bony ostium.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The question is really about **timing**, and timing is what examiners test on dacryocystitis, because it is where candidates lose marks and where patients lose eyes. Settle the infection first — compresses, systemic antibiotics, no probing and no irrigation (`opqb-t4-154`) — then operate on the quiet sac, since the block that caused the infection has not gone anywhere.\n\n*(For where the DCR ostium is actually made, see `opqb-t4-139`: the **middle** meatus.)*",
    objective:'DCR is indicated for chronic dacryocystitis, a mucocele and a lacrimal fistula, but is contraindicated during acute dacryocystitis — it is deferred until the infection settles.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.23 (Q160), key p.24 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-161', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'Several ducts pass from the **orbital portion of the lacrimal gland** and open into:',
    options:['The lateral aspect of the superior fornix.',
             'The medial aspect of the superior fornix.',
             'The medial aspect of the inferior fornix.',
             'The lateral aspect of the inferior fornix.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The lacrimal gland sits in the lacrimal fossa in the upper OUTER corner of the orbit, so its ducts run down and open where they are — the lateral part of the superior fornix.** Roughly ten to twelve ducts leave the **orbital** lobe, pass through or alongside the **palpebral** lobe, and discharge into the superior conjunctival fornix laterally, from where the blink sweeps the tear medially across the eye toward the puncta.\n\n**⚠️ This exact fact is a gap in the course material, and it is worth stating precisely what is and is not there.** Book ch.1 p.14 gets closest: \"**The main lacrimal gland** which secretes tears, **and its excretory ducts, which convey the fluid to the ocular surface**. It is located in the **lacrimal fossa which lies anteriorly in the upper temporal part of the orbit**.\" It names the ducts and stops — no fornix, no side. Book ch.4 p.63 does not mention the ducts in prose at all; its lacrimal-apparatus figure carries the labels **\"Orbital part of lacrimal gland\"**, **\"Palpebral part of lacrimal gland\"**, **\"Levator palpebrae superioris\"**, **\"Lacrimal ducts\"** and **\"Conjunctival sac\"**, and the cache records that the orbital/palpebral division \"exists ONLY in this figure and nowhere in the chapter’s prose\". `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 4 labels \"Lacrimal gland\" on a sagittal diagram and, in its own not-covered list, records the absence explicitly: \"the **ORBITAL and PALPEBRAL LOBES** of the lacrimal gland **by name**, its **INNERVATION**… and its blood supply. Slide 4 labels ‘Lacrimal gland’ on a diagram and **stops there**.\" `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy…` slide 52 labels \"Lacrimal gland (orbital part)\" and \"Lacrimal gland (palpebral part)\" on the check-ligament diagram — again a label, with no duct and no fornix. *(The number of ducts, their course through the palpebral lobe and the lateral superior fornix as their destination are therefore **not taken from the course material**. They are standard, uncontested orbital anatomy, given because the bank asks for them.)*\n\n**The one thing the material does supply is the reasoning**, and it is enough to get the answer without the outside fact: the gland is in the **upper temporal** orbit (book ch.1 p.14), the **superior** fornix is the upper recess of the conjunctival sac, and *temporal* is *lateral*. Upper and outer gland → upper and outer fornix.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What is actually there |\n|---|---|\n| **Medial aspect of the superior fornix** | The correct fornix, the wrong end. The **medial** end of the palpebral aperture is the **drainage** side — the puncta, canaliculi and sac. Tears are made laterally and leave medially; the whole of `L9)` slide 8’s \"tear out flow\" figure is arrows running **medially** across the eye toward the puncta. |\n| **Medial aspect of the inferior fornix** | Wrong on both counts. |\n| **Lateral aspect of the inferior fornix** | Right side, wrong fornix. The inferior fornix does contain **accessory** lacrimal tissue — `L9)` slide 4 labels \"Glands of Krause\" inferiorly as well as superiorly — but the main gland’s ducts do not reach it. |\n\n**⚠️ Transcription note on the source.** The bank prints option A as \"the superior **formix**\" — a typographical fault for *fornix*, and it sits on the keyed option. Options B, C and D all print \"fornix\" correctly. Verified at 320 dpi on book p.23; the stem is transcribed here with the misprint repaired, since it is a printing fault and not a content difference.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The lobes are joined and the ducts are shared, which has a surgical consequence worth knowing: because the orbital lobe’s ducts pass through the palpebral lobe, **removing or damaging the palpebral lobe obstructs the drainage of the whole gland**, so a biopsy of a lacrimal gland mass is taken from the **orbital** lobe. It is also the palpebral lobe you can see: evert the upper lid and look laterally, and an enlarged gland — the S-shaped ptosis of `opqb-t4-155` — is visible there.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The lacrimal gland lies in the upper temporal orbit and its ducts open into the lateral part of the superior conjunctival fornix; tears are made laterally and drain medially.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.23 (Q161), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-162', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'**Tear film break-up time** measures:',
    options:['Tear film production.','Tear film distribution.','Tear film volume.','Tear film stability.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Tear film breakup time (TBUT)** normally **more than 10 seconds** (if less, it is an indication of an **unstable tear film**).\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim, including its \"more than10\" set without a space. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The box is a near-quotation of book p.65**, which lists among the signs of dry eye disease: \"**Decreased tear film breakup time (TBUT)** normally **more than 10 seconds** (if less, it is an indication of an **unstable tear film**)\" — and the word *unstable* is the answer.\n\n**How it is done**, from the book’s own tutorial box on p.65: **fluorescein** (yellow) is instilled, the film is viewed under **cobalt blue light** (\"yellow + blue = green\"), and the timing runs from a **complete blink to the appearance of the first dry spot (black spot)** in the green film. **Normal ≥ 10 seconds.** A dry spot appearing at five seconds means the film cannot hold together between blinks.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and each is measured by something else:**\n\n| Option | What actually measures it |\n|---|---|\n| **Tear film production** | **Schirmer’s test** — millimetres of a filter paper strip wetted in five minutes (`opqb-t4-140`). Production and stability are different properties and can be discordant, which is the entire basis of `opqb-t4-149`: TBUT 5 s with Schirmer 14 mm. |\n| **Tear film distribution** | Not a measured quantity in this material. Distribution is the **blink’s** job, and where it fails — lid malposition, lagophthalmos, infrequent blinking — the abnormality shows up **as** a short TBUT rather than as a measurement of its own. |\n| **Tear film volume** | The **marginal tear strip** height, which `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 13 says \"is examined on the slit lamp prior to any manipulation of the eyelids or instillation of topical medication\" and gives as \"**0.2–0.4 mm normally**\" — the only tear-volume figure in either source. |\n\n**⚠️ Where each source teaches this, because they differ.** The book makes TBUT the dry-eye test and never mentions Schirmer’s. `L9)`, the lacrimal lecture, does the reverse of neither — it teaches the **fluorescein disappearance test** for drainage (slide 13) and records in its own not-covered list that \"**TEAR BREAK-UP TIME (BUT/TBUT)**. Absent — the tear film is taught as **three layers with thicknesses** (slide 3) and **never as a stability measurement**.\" So the fact this question tests comes from the **book**, and only from the book. `L15,16) Cornea` names TBUT once more, in the pre-refractive-surgery workup: \"Adequate tear film (Schirmer’s, TBUT)\".\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** TBUT is the cheapest, fastest dry-eye test there is — a fluorescein strip, a blue light and a count — and a short one is what tells you the film is failing even when the volume is normal. That combination points at the **lipid** layer and therefore at the **lid margin** (`opqb-t4-150`, `opqb-t4-159`), which is where the treatment goes.",
    objective:'TBUT measures tear film stability — normally more than 10 seconds; Schirmer measures production and the marginal tear strip measures volume.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.23 (Q162), key p.24 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-163', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'Mother noticed in her **3-month-old** infant continuous epiphora and frequent muco-purulent discharge. Your advice for primary treatment should be:',
    options:['Syringing.','Antibiotics and massage.','Dacryo-cyst-ectomy.','Dacryo-cysto-rhinostomy.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction in a three-month-old, and the primary treatment is the bottom rung again: lacrimal sac massage, with antibiotics for the mucopurulent discharge.** Book p.67: \"**Digital massage of the lacrimal sac with topical antibiotics** before the age of one year (**85% resolve within the first year**).\" The full escalation ladder, the mechanism by which massage works, and the recorded divergence between this bank’s six-month window and the book’s twelve are set out in `opqb-t4-141` and are not repeated here.\n\n**The discharge is expected, not alarming.** `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 21 lists exactly this presentation: \"**epiphora and matting of eyelashes** may be constant or intermittent and may be particularly noticeable when the child has an upper respiratory tract infection\", \"**superimposed bacterial conjunctivitis**\", and \"gentle pressure over the lacrimal sac may cause **mucopurulent reflux**\" — while \"**acute dacryocystitis is very rare**\". So the antibiotic here treats the secondary conjunctivitis; the massage treats the block.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not at three months |\n|---|---|\n| **Syringing** | Irrigating an infant’s passages is not the primary treatment, and `L9)` slide 15 attaches two cautions to it: \"lacrimal **irrigation** should be performed only after ascertaining punctal patency\" and probing \"is **contraindicated in acute infection**\". It is a step for a duct that has not opened by itself, not a first advice to a mother. |\n| **Dacryocystectomy** | **Removal of the sac** — nowhere on the congenital ladder, and it destroys drainage permanently in a child whose drainage is very likely about to work by itself. *(Not taken from the course material — the word appears in no cached ophthalmology deck and in no book chapter.)* |\n| **Dacryocystorhinostomy** | The **last** rung — book p.67 places it after massage, after probing repeated once, and after silastic intubation. A bone-opening operation on a three-month-old with an 85 % chance of spontaneous cure. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The differential is short but it must be run: `L9)` slide 21 lists \"other congenital causes of a watering eye, such as **punctal atresia, congenital glaucoma, chronic conjunctivitis (e.g. chlamydial)**\". **Congenital glaucoma is the one that must not be missed** — a watering, photophobic infant with a large or hazy cornea is not a blocked duct, and delay costs sight. Also look for the **bluish cystic swelling** of a congenital dacryocoele at or below the medial canthus (`L9)` slide 23), which can obstruct the nose and cause respiratory distress.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A 3-month-old with epiphora and mucopurulent discharge is managed with lacrimal sac massage and topical antibiotics — never syringing or surgery as the primary step.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.23 (Q163), key p.24' },

  { id:'opqb-t4-164', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lac',
    stem:'A **14-month-old** child presented with recurrent redness and discharge in the left eye **since birth**. **Regurge test was positive.** Treatment should be in the form of:',
    options:['Long term use of antibiotics.','Massage to the lacrimal passages.','Probing of the lacrimal passages.','Dacryo-cysto-rhinostomy'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The same congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction as `opqb-t4-141`, `opqb-t4-143` and `opqb-t4-163` — but this child is fourteen months old, past the age at which the conservative rung is abandoned, so the answer moves up to probing.**\n\n**The age is the whole question, and the book gives the threshold twice.** Book p.67: \"(a) Digital massage of the lacrimal sac with topical antibiotics **before the age of one year** (85% resolve within the first year); (b) **Nasolacrimal duct probing after the age of one year.** It can be **repeated only once** if the first attempt was not curative.\" This child is **14 months**, so massage has had its chance and the 85 % window has closed. Note that fourteen months clears **both** thresholds in play in this topic — the book’s twelve months and this bank’s own six (see the divergence recorded in `opqb-t4-141`) — so no ambiguity arises here.\n\n**Everything else in the stem confirms the diagnosis rather than complicating it.** *Since birth* makes it congenital. *Recurrent redness and discharge* is the \"superimposed bacterial conjunctivitis\" of `L9) lacrimal system.` slide 21. A **positive regurge test** — reflux on pressure over the sac — is the finding book p.66 assigns to \"patent upper lacrimal system / **NLD obstruction** / chronic dacryocystitis\", and `L9)` slide 12 reads as \"indicative of a **patent canalicular system, but with an obstruction distal to the lower end of the lacrimal sac**\". Patent above, blocked below: that is exactly the lesion a probe is designed to reach.\n\n**What probing actually does.** `L9)` slide 22: \"**Probing.** Passage of a fine wire via the canalicular system and nasolacrimal duct to **disrupt the obstructive membrane at the valve of Hasner**, followed by **irrigation to confirm the patency**.\" Slide 21 explains why the membrane is there at all — the lower end of the duct at the valve of Hasner \"is the **last portion** of the lacrimal drainage system to canalize\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not at fourteen months |\n|---|---|\n| **Long term use of antibiotics** | Treats the secondary conjunctivitis and never the obstruction. The discharge returns the moment the drops stop, because tears are still stagnating. It is not on either source’s ladder. |\n| **Massage to the lacrimal passages** | The correct answer **before** one year (`opqb-t4-141`, `opqb-t4-163`) and the wrong one after it. Its chance has passed; continuing it just defers definitive treatment. |\n| **Dacryocystorhinostomy** | Two rungs too far. Book p.67 places DCR after a **repeated** probing and then **silastic intubation** have both failed — and it adds the line worth remembering for adults: \"**N.B: Probing is not curative in adults.**\" |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The congenital ladder is short, ordered and heavily examined: **massage → probe (repeat once) → intubate → DCR**, with the hinge at **one year**. Get the age right and the question answers itself; the four questions this bank builds on it differ in almost nothing else.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Congenital NLDO persisting past one year is treated by probing of the lacrimal passages — massage is for the first year, and DCR only after probing and intubation fail.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.23 (Q164), key p.24' }
